# synchronized, volatile, locks, conditions, and atomics

## Guard one invariant consistently
```java
final class Counter {
    private int value;
    synchronized void increment() { value++; }
    synchronized int value() { return value; }
}
```
Both access paths acquire the same monitor. Synchronization provides mutual exclusion and visibility. Synchronizing writes but leaving reads unprotected can still violate the visibility contract.

volatile makes reads/writes visible with ordering guarantees but does not make count++ atomic. AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet handles one atomic update. Several related atomics do not automatically protect a multi-field invariant such as transferring a balance.

ReentrantLock supports interruptible/timed acquisition; unlock belongs in finally. Conditions require a loop checking the predicate because waking does not guarantee the desired state. Acquire multiple locks in a consistent order to avoid deadlock cycles.

## Practice
Repair a counter with synchronized, then AtomicInteger, and explain both contracts. Design a two-field invariant and show why independent atomic increments are insufficient. Add a bounded lock acquisition policy. Do not hold locks across slow network operations without a compelling ownership reason.

# Virtual threads, concurrent collections, semaphores, and latches

## Virtual threads suit blocking workloads
```java
try (var executor = java.util.concurrent.Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    var result = executor.submit(() -> "loaded");
    System.out.println(result.get());
}
```
Virtual threads are supported in Java 21. They reduce the cost of many waiting threads, not the CPU cost of computation. Do not pool virtual threads merely to limit database access; bound the scarce downstream resource with a semaphore or connection pool. Java 21 has pinning limitations, including blocking while holding certain monitors, so measure the target runtime.

## Concurrent structures
ConcurrentHashMap supports thread-safe operations, but get-then-put is still a compound protocol; use compute/merge where appropriate. BlockingQueue combines transfer with waiting. CountDownLatch waits for a fixed count; Semaphore bounds permits. CopyOnWriteArrayList favors frequent iteration and rare mutation because each write copies storage.

## Practice
Design 100 independent blocking tasks with at most 5 simultaneous dependency calls. Release permits in finally and preserve interruption. Explain how unbounded task submission can still consume excessive memory despite cheap virtual threads. Avoid timing-sensitive tests based only on sleeps.

